---
//! Answers for people choosing a streaming setup. Platform and network
//! limits follow README.md, docs/parity.md and docs/networking.md.
import { onGitHub } from '../lib/repo';
---

<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
      <h2 class="title">Before you stream</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="questions">
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Can I use pingpong for remote desktop gaming?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Pong streams the host’s desktop, including games, to Ping on another computer. It supports up to
          4K and 120 fps, HEVC or H.264 video, and stereo to 7.1 sound. Controllers and rumble work with Windows
          hosts; macOS hosts do not support controllers yet. Your hardware and network affect performance.
          See the <a href="#latency">benchmarks and their test conditions</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Is pingpong an alternative to Moonlight and Sunshine/Apollo?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Ping takes the client’s role and Pong takes the host’s role. Install them together: pingpong uses
          its own protocol, so Ping does not connect to Sunshine or Apollo, and Moonlight does not
          connect to Pong. Read the <a href={onGitHub('docs/parity.md')}>feature comparison with Moonlight and
          Sunshine/Apollo</a>
          for the supported features and limits.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Which computers can host and receive a stream?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Both apps run on macOS 14 or later, Windows 10 and 11, and Linux under X11 or Wayland. A Windows host
          needs an NVIDIA GPU and SudoVDA. Linux hosts stream a scaled screen without a virtual display; on
          sway and Hyprland, the client cannot send keyboard or mouse input. Phones, tablets and TVs are out of
          scope. Check the <a href="#platforms">platform requirements</a> before downloading.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Do I need a VPN or port forwarding to stream over the internet?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Pair Ping and Pong once on your local network, then enable internet access on the host. pingpong
          finds paired hosts through sealed records on the BitTorrent DHT and connects directly by punching
          through NATs. It has no relay fallback: symmetric NAT on both sides can prevent a connection. In
          that case, forward UDP port 47800 to the host. See
          <a href={onGitHub('docs/networking.md')}>internet setup and network limits</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Is pingpong free and open source?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Ping and Pong are free to download and use, with their source code available under the GPL-3.0
          license. Install Pong on the computer you stream from and Ping on the one you sit at.
          <a href="#install">Download Ping and Pong</a> for your system.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .questions {
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  details {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    padding-block: 20px;
  }

  details:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2);
  }

  summary {
    color: var(--text);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  summary::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  h3 {
    display: inline;
    font-size: 1rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  details p {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
